16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 19–23, 1864","place":"Spotsylvania Court House to the North Anna River and Hanover Junction, Virginia","result":"Grant’s attempt to draw Lee into attacking an isolated II Corps failed; Lee recognized the general movement and withdrew directly to the North Anna, where the armies prepared for another engagement","facts":[{"label":"Ewell’s force at Harris Farm","value":"About 6,000"},{"label":"U.S. Harris Farm casualties","value":"About 1,500"},{"label":"Confederate Harris Farm casualties","value":"About 900"},{"label":"Heavy-artillery regiments in Tyler’s division","value":"5","note":"Averaging about 1,500 men each"},{"label":"Milford Station from Spotsylvania","value":"About 17 miles"},{"label":"Hancock’s separation from the main army","value":"About 20 miles"},{"label":"North Anna width","value":"About 150 feet"},{"label":"Confederate reinforcements received","value":"More than 10,000"}],"maps":[{"title":"The movement from Spotsylvania to North Anna","caption":"The campaign map shows Grant’s southeastern route through Guinea Station, Milford Station, and Bowling Green while Lee used the more direct Telegraph Road toward Hanover Junction.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign movement from Spotsylvania to North Anna"},{"title":"The North Anna and Hanover Junction","caption":"The battle plan identifies the river crossings and the two railroads meeting at Hanover Junction. Lee needed to protect the Virginia Central route to the Shenandoah Valley.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00237/","image":"/images/north-anna-battle-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the North Anna River and Hanover Junction"},{"title":"Spotsylvania, Milford Station, and the North Anna today","caption":"Hancock moved well east of the main army before turning south. The two armies then marched on roughly parallel routes toward the North Anna.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.85%2C37.75%2C-77.20%2C38.30&layer=mapnik&marker=37.9008%2C-77.4995"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:18","audioTime":78,"title":"Grant prepared another southeastern movement","detail":"Defeats in the Shenandoah Valley and at Bermuda Hundred freed Confederate reinforcements for Lee. Grant concluded that remaining at Spotsylvania offered no further advantage."},{"time":"3:12","audioTime":192,"title":"The army needed a new supply base","detail":"Moving around Lee’s right exposed the road back to Belle Plain. Grant designated Port Royal on the Rappahannock for future supplies and reinforcements."},{"time":"3:53","audioTime":233,"title":"Grant wanted an open-field battle","detail":"The North Anna and Richmond fortifications offered Lee new defensive positions. Grant sought a way to make Lee leave his earthworks."},{"time":"5:40","audioTime":340,"title":"Hancock’s II Corps became the bait","detail":"Grant planned to move II Corps around the Confederate right while the rest of the army remained at Spotsylvania. If Lee attacked Hancock, Grant intended to strike the committed Confederates."},{"time":"6:31","audioTime":391,"title":"A Confederate movement delayed the plan","detail":"On May 19 U.S. formations shifted to a north-south line while Hancock assembled in the rear. Confederate troops appeared near Harris Farm before Hancock could depart."},{"time":"7:26","audioTime":446,"title":"The Harris Farm action threatened the supply road","detail":"Confederates approached the Fredericksburg Road on the U.S. right. Meade ordered Hancock to reinforce the sector and postponed the turning movement."},{"time":"8:24","audioTime":504,"title":"Grant ordered a counterattack","detail":"He sent Horace Porter to the threatened flank with instructions to stop the advance and take the offensive, with Warren’s corps supporting."},{"time":"9:02","audioTime":542,"title":"Former heavy artillerists entered their first battle","detail":"Robert Tyler’s division contained five large regiments trained for Washington’s forts. Grant had recently converted them to infantry duty."},{"time":"11:13","audioTime":673,"title":"Ewell’s reconnaissance became a battle","detail":"Lee ordered Ewell to locate the new U.S. flank. Ewell used his entire six-thousand-man corps, and Ramseur began an attack after mistakenly expecting the U.S. troops to charge."},{"time":"12:19","audioTime":739,"title":"Three hours of fighting followed","detail":"The battle took place in heavy rain. Confederate troops briefly reached a wagon train but could not hold the ground against Tyler and additional heavy-artillery regiments."},{"time":"13:32","audioTime":812,"title":"Both sides suffered heavy casualties","detail":"Ewell withdrew after losing about 900 men. The U.S. heavy-artillery regiments lost about 1,500 but established their ability to fight as infantry."},{"time":"14:14","audioTime":854,"title":"Grant still believed Lee might attack","detail":"The Harris Farm action increased Grant’s hope that an apparently isolated II Corps would draw Lee away from his entrenchments."},{"time":"14:45","audioTime":885,"title":"Hancock began marching on May 20","detail":"II Corps moved east after dark with cavalry protection, reached Guinea Station, and then followed the railroad south."},{"time":"15:11","audioTime":911,"title":"Hancock reached Milford Station and Bowling Green","detail":"Cavalry reached Milford Station by 9 a.m. on May 21. The lead infantry crossed the Mattaponi River near Bowling Green around noon."},{"time":"16:02","audioTime":962,"title":"II Corps was about twenty miles from support","detail":"Hancock threatened Lee’s communications but remained isolated. Prisoners from Pickett’s division showed that reinforcements had already reached the area."},{"time":"16:55","audioTime":1015,"title":"Hancock stopped and fortified","detail":"The presence of Pickett’s troops made a farther unsupported advance too dangerous. II Corps entrenched near the Mattaponi and waited."},{"time":"18:26","audioTime":1106,"title":"Lee did not take the bait","detail":"He interpreted Hancock’s march as the leading element of a general U.S. movement, not an isolated target, and ordered the Army of Northern Virginia toward the North Anna."},{"time":"19:21","audioTime":1161,"title":"The armies marched on parallel routes","detail":"For about thirty-six hours Grant moved through Guinea and Milford while Lee took the Telegraph Road. The armies were out of direct contact for the first time since May 5."},{"time":"20:21","audioTime":1221,"title":"The armies entered largely untouched Caroline County","detail":"U.S. soldiers found livestock and crops in a wealthy agricultural area. Unauthorized foraging and pillaging increased during the march."},{"time":"21:52","audioTime":1312,"title":"Officers disagreed about foraging","detail":"Some punished pillagers and threatened severe penalties; others considered the behavior inevitable. Grant allowed authorized foraging but treated straggling and theft as threats to discipline."},{"time":"24:09","audioTime":1449,"title":"Lee selected the North Anna as a temporary shield","detail":"The river protected Hanover Junction and was crossable at only a few places. Its higher north bank, however, allowed U.S. artillery to command many crossings."},{"time":"26:21","audioTime":1581,"title":"Lee expected Grant to move toward Richmond","detail":"Lee thought Grant would cross the Pamunkey and approach from the east or rebuild the railroad for a direct southern advance. He did not expect immediate battle at North Anna."},{"time":"29:18","audioTime":1758,"title":"Lee misidentified Grant’s main objective","detail":"Confederate leaders assumed Richmond was the objective. Grant’s operational goal remained the destruction of the Army of Northern Virginia."},{"time":"30:53","audioTime":1853,"title":"Lee asked to concentrate with Beauregard","detail":"With more than 10,000 reinforcements and shorter supply lines, Lee told Davis on May 23 that combining forces might permit a decisive attack on Grant."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Planned to use II Corps to draw Lee into open battle and ordered the army southeast"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Redirected Hancock to Harris Farm before the march began"},{"commander":"Winfield S. Hancock","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Advanced through Guinea and Milford Stations to the Mattaponi River"},{"commander":"Robert O. Tyler","command":"Heavy Artillery Division","subordinates":"Five converted heavy-artillery regiments that fought at Harris Farm"},{"commander":"Gouverneur K. Warren","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"Supported the right flank and followed Hancock during the general movement"},{"commander":"Marsena R. Patrick","command":"Provost marshal general","subordinates":"Responsible for controlling stragglers and unauthorized pillaging"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Ordered the North Anna withdrawal and sought concentration with Beauregard"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Used his corps to locate the new U.S. flank at Harris Farm"},{"commander":"Stephen D. Ramseur","command":"Brigade, Second Corps","subordinates":"Opened the Harris Farm attack after misreading U.S. intentions"},{"commander":"George E. Pickett","command":"Division, First Corps","subordinates":"Returning troops whose presence warned Hancock that reinforcements were arriving"},{"commander":"P. G. T. Beauregard","command":"Department of North Carolina and Southern Virginia","subordinates":"Commanded forces Lee hoped to combine with his army"}]}],"people":[{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Designed the attempted trap and shifted the army toward North Anna after it failed"},{"name":"Winfield S. Hancock","role":"Led the isolated II Corps movement intended to draw Lee into attacking","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert O. Tyler","role":"Commanded former heavy artillerists in their first infantry battle at Harris Farm","side":"United States"},{"name":"Horace Porter","role":"Grant staff officer sent to report from Harris Farm and encourage a counterattack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Recognized the wider U.S. movement and withdrew to protect Hanover Junction","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"},{"name":"Richard S. Ewell","role":"Conducted the reconnaissance that became the costly Harris Farm battle","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Stephen D. Ramseur","role":"Began the Confederate attack after incorrectly expecting Tyler’s troops to charge","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Walter H. Taylor","role":"Lee’s adjutant who recorded Confederate expectations about Grant’s next movement","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign route","caption":"The armies left the Spotsylvania lines and moved southeast toward the North Anna on separate roads.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"image":"/images/north-anna-battle-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the North Anna River and Hanover Junction","caption":"The North Anna protected the rail junction that connected Lee to Richmond and the Shenandoah Valley.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00237/"},{"image":"/images/jericho-mill-pontoon-bridge.jpg","alt":"1864 photograph of a canvas pontoon bridge at Jericho Mill","caption":"The 50th New York Engineers built this bridge for Warren’s V Corps during the North Anna operations.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2011660470/"},{"image":"/images/birney-crossing-north-anna.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of U.S. troops crossing the North Anna","caption":"The river could be crossed at only a limited number of bridges and fords, making their control essential.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660416/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 491","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/05/05/civil-war-podcast-episode-491/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Spotsylvania Court House history","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/learn/historyculture/spotsylvania.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Guinea Station and the 1864 movement","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/guinea-station-history-fairfield-during-the-civil-war.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of North Anna","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/planyourvisit/bona.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Overland Campaign","url":"https://history.army.mil/Publications/Publications-Catalog/Overland-Campaign/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Overland Campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: North Anna battle plan","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00237/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Jericho Mill canvas pontoon bridge","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2011660470/"}]}}]}]
